  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 306 PM EDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\n- Showers and Storms Saturday into Sunday\n\n There have been some shifts in the trends for tomorrow's incoming\nsystem. Today, the showers and storms continue to stream through\nIllinois and Indiana. This trend was expected to shift northward \nbut there is some question to what extent.\n\n An upper level vort max is still shown to move through Lower \nMichigan tomorrow, This would create some afternoon into early \nevening convection along and southward of the I 94 corridor. This \nis highlighted in the SPC Day 2 convective outlook. Latest Model \nsoundings through the Kalamazoo region so lapse rates only 5 to 6 \nC/km with a mid level inversion tomorrow that could stifle any \nconvection. Some of the ensembles do show some weak shear Saturday\nevening that shifts to the north and could allow some showers and\nstorms along the I 96 corridor Saturday evening. So scattered \nshowers and storms are possible, there is the possibility any \nconvection remains to the south. \n The overall trend continues to show a shift of stronger\nconvection southward. There remains a chance for increased\nprecipitation through the south and given the moisture aloft,\nany showers could by heavy, with QPF of over an inch of rain\npossible. \n\nThere is a deeper upper level trough with mid level \nsupport moving through early Sunday. While there is less moisture \nwith that system and it comes in overnight into early Sunday, it \ncurrently has the better CAPE and shear. While not huge, there is \n1500 J/kg of MU cape and Lapse rates towards 7 C/km. This is with \nthe frontal boundary and propagates through between 15Z to 21Z \nSunday. So there remains a multitude of questions with the systems\nthis weekend, there remains several chances for showers and \nstorms across lower Michigan until Sunday evening but the trend\nshows a lower potential. \n\n- Cool and Dry through beginning of the week\n\n There is good consistency that this front moves through Sunday\nnight with drier, cooler northwesterly flow into Monday. \n A large upper level low over Canada will dominate the pattern\nthrough the next week. It should form into a closed low over\nHudson Bay through mid week. \n This will keep temps at to below normal with the region dry. \n\n- Showers and storms possible mid next week\n\n The previously mentioned upper level low will drive a short wave\ntrough through the region and a corresponding low through the\nGreat Lakes midweek. Currently there should be an elongating front\nassociated with with warm air advection out ahead of it. There \nremains some position and timing questions. It could remain \nfurther north and/or come in later. But Wednesday into Thursday \ncould see some decent convection.",
